From d8fd6246b4e3615d4dee33165636dd6902dbea96 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 23:33:20 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] [Qwen4Exp] on-demand host-RAM offload for the vision encoder

Serving Qwen3.8-Flash-Next on one 96 GiB card sizes the KV cache from
whatever VRAM the weights leave behind, so a vision tower that is idle for
essentially every forward pass is still charged for 100% of the time. The
escape hatch we had been using, --language-model-only, buys that VRAM back
by deleting multimodal support outright.

This keeps the tower's weights in pinned host RAM and pays PCIe only while
an image is actually being encoded.

  --mm-encoder-offload onload   (default "none": nothing changes)

"onload" installs a forward pre-hook/hook pair on each selected submodule:
the pre-hook copies that submodule's parameters to the device just before it
runs, the hook drops them again. Device residency is one submodule at a time
(40.5 MiB peak for this checkpoint), not the whole tower. "uva" is offered
for a compiled or CUDA-graph-captured encoder, where the pointer swapping
that "onload" does would be baked into the graph; resolve_mm_encoder_offload_mode
raises rather than let that combination produce wrong pixels.

Selection is deliberately narrow: only modules that own parameters directly
(so containers are never double-counted), only above 1 MiB (norms stay put),
and only under an explicit prefix allowlist. patch_embed and pos_embed are
excluded on purpose -- pos_embed.weight is handed straight to a Triton kernel
and patch_embed backs the tower's .dtype/.device properties, so neither
reaches its parameters through nn.Module.__call__. For
mazinb/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-Uncensored-NVFP4 that offloads 888,759,008 of the
tower's 897,862,112 bytes and leaves 8.68 MiB resident.

The hook runs from the model-level process_weights_after_loading, not from
load_weights: every layer's quant_method.process_weights_after_loading runs
after load_weights and may repack or replace a parameter, and
device_loading_context would drag a host-resident parameter back onto the
device and hand it back as a fresh unpinned CPU tensor.

Tests are CPU-only: module selection against a meta-device replica of the
real tower (names, shapes and dtype exact), the onload/free lifecycle with a
stand-in device copy, and a config-resolution test that runs serve.sh under
DRY_RUN=1 and checks the argv it builds actually parses to the documented
EngineArgs -- serve.sh hardcodes --limit-mm-per-prompt {"image":0,"video":0}
and word-splits EXTRA_ARGS after it, so both "later occurrence wins" and
"the JSON survives unquoted expansion" are load-bearing.

